WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:02.420
- [Narrator] This is the Facebook logo.

00:00:02.420 --> 00:00:04.180
This is a pile of tobacco.

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:05.013
Why?

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:08.620
Because at the recent Facebook hearings,

00:00:08.620 --> 00:00:10.710
we've heard a lot of this.

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.950
- Facebook is just like Big Tobacco,

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.900
pushing a product that
they know is harmful

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:18.410
to the health of young people.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:21.550
- Facebook has taken
Big Tobacco's playbook.

00:00:21.550 --> 00:00:22.770
- The tobacco industry--

00:00:22.770 --> 00:00:23.990
- Big Tobacco--
Big Tobacco--

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:25.310
- All right. So you get it.

00:00:25.310 --> 00:00:28.160
Members of Congress really
like this comparison,

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.270
especially around the impacts and harms

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:32.050
of social media on kids.

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:34.330
And if you look back at
the hearings of the 1990s,

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:35.470
where the chief executives

00:00:35.470 --> 00:00:37.840
of the biggest tobacco
companies testified,

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:40.200
you can see why it's a handy comparison,

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:42.810
but as I'll explain, an imperfect one.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.340
- Do you believe nicotine
is not addictive?

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:47.310
- I believe nicotine
is not addictive, yes.

00:00:47.310 --> 00:00:51.460
- Congressmen, cigarettes
and nicotine clearly do not

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:54.110
meet the classic definitions of addiction.

00:00:54.110 --> 00:00:55.720
- I don't believe that nicotine

00:00:55.720 --> 00:00:56.830
or our products are addictive.

00:00:56.830 --> 00:00:58.950
- I believe that it is not addictive.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:01.540
- [Narrator] And here we are
more than 25 years later.

00:01:01.540 --> 00:01:03.990
- We certainly do not want
our products to be addictive.

00:01:03.990 --> 00:01:07.490
- So I disagree with calling
our product addictive.

00:01:07.490 --> 00:01:09.810
I also think that's not
how we build products.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:12.430
- In our recent Wall Street
Journal, Facebook File series,

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:15.130
my colleagues revealed
internal company documents

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:18.160
that show that the company
knows its products cause harm,

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:19.880
including to kids.

00:01:19.880 --> 00:01:21.440
Okay.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:22.800
Factor tobacco.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:25.220
Now, while it's a convenient analogy,

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.523
in some ways, it goes up in smoke.

00:01:28.550 --> 00:01:30.860
For one, the tobacco
companies spent decades

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.230
concealing their research,

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:34.610
lying and covering up what they knew

00:01:34.610 --> 00:01:36.630
and even more important, social media

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.350
does not cause lung cancer.

00:01:38.350 --> 00:01:39.910
- The truth is that cigarettes

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:44.490
are the single most dangerous
consumer product ever sold.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.410
- That's former democratic
Congressman Henry Waxman.

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:50.460
He led the Big Tobacco
hearings in the 1990s.

00:01:50.460 --> 00:01:52.040
This is him now.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:54.560
So tobacco and social media,

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:56.270
what do you make of this comparison?

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.280
- There's nothing good about tobacco.

00:01:58.280 --> 00:02:01.440
It's the only legal product we have

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.250
that we know that kills
and makes people sick

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:08.560
and has no redeeming value.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:13.140
Social media has pluses
and some big minuses.

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:15.787
- Facebook spokesman, Joe
Osborne, said in a statement,

00:02:15.787 --> 00:02:17.470
"It's an absurd comparison.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.230
Social media helps people connect

00:02:19.230 --> 00:02:21.080
and small businesses thrive."

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:23.280
But looking at how lawmakers
came up with regulation

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:25.470
for this box may give us some clues

00:02:25.470 --> 00:02:28.500
about how they might handle this box.

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:29.333
But to do that,

00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:31.630
we have to look at the
similarities between the two.

00:02:31.630 --> 00:02:34.550
The first we can call, hooking for profit.

00:02:34.550 --> 00:02:36.690
The more addicted you are to smoking,

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:38.700
the more money tobacco companies make.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:41.840
The more time you spend
scrolling Facebook or Instagram

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:43.560
and thus consuming ads,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.020
the more money Facebook makes.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:47.070
In the 1990s,

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:49.850
the tobacco companies denied
adjusting nicotine levels

00:02:49.850 --> 00:02:51.470
to encourage addiction.

00:02:51.470 --> 00:02:54.000
- The claim that Phillip
Morris secretly adds nicotine

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:57.770
during the manufacturing
process to keep smokers addicted

00:02:57.770 --> 00:02:59.040
is false.

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.920
- [Narrator] And here we are in 2021.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:03.810
- But I want to drill down
on this addictive element.

00:03:03.810 --> 00:03:07.890
But isn't part of your business
model to have more eyeballs

00:03:07.890 --> 00:03:12.890
for a longer amount of time
engaged using your services?

00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:15.333
- Respectfully, Senator,

00:03:15.333 --> 00:03:17.880
that's not actually how
we build our products--

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.990
- [Narrator] Then there's
the not sharing of research.

00:03:19.990 --> 00:03:21.840
- You're the Chief Executive Officer.

00:03:22.700 --> 00:03:26.010
What reason could you not
give us this research,

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.810
if it's been conducted?

00:03:28.810 --> 00:03:31.660
- I have no problem giving
you any material we have,

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:33.400
if it's not in some way involved

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.400
in active litigation at this time.

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:37.650
- [Narrator] And here we are today.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.800
- Will you disclose all of
the reports, all of the finds.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:42.910
Will you commit to full disclosure?

00:03:42.910 --> 00:03:46.470
- Yeah, I know that we have
released a number of the reports

00:03:46.470 --> 00:03:49.590
and we are looking to find ways to release

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.770
more of this research.

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.070
I want to be clear that this
research is not a bombshell.

00:03:55.070 --> 00:03:57.053
It's not causal research.

00:03:57.053 --> 00:04:00.000
- This research is a bombshell.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:00.833
- Now again,

00:04:00.833 --> 00:04:03.910
the tobacco companies had
years of scientific evidence

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.180
of health impacts of their product.

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:08.640
In Facebook's case, the
company is in early days

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.460
of understanding the impacts
of its digital tools.

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:14.440
And it has already
shared some information.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:16.220
And that's perhaps the
biggest link being made

00:04:16.220 --> 00:04:18.410
between Big Tobacco and Facebook.

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:20.420
Hooking the kids early.

00:04:20.420 --> 00:04:23.110
Though, I guess you could
say that of other companies

00:04:23.110 --> 00:04:24.470
like junk food.

00:04:24.470 --> 00:04:25.890
Maybe we should have done this whole video

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.500
about Facebook and junk food.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:30.409
The most famous tobacco targeting to kids?

00:04:30.409 --> 00:04:31.433
- (indistinct) medical association found

00:04:31.433 --> 00:04:35.180
that 91% of the children,
three through six,

00:04:35.180 --> 00:04:38.330
could match Joe with a Camel cigarette,

00:04:38.330 --> 00:04:39.790
which means, Mr. Johnston,

00:04:39.790 --> 00:04:42.910
the Joe Camel was as well
known as Mickey Mouse.

00:04:42.910 --> 00:04:45.610
Do you still maintain
that Joe Camel cartoons

00:04:45.610 --> 00:04:47.373
are not targeted towards children?

00:04:49.329 --> 00:04:51.427
- Congressman Synar, when--

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.880
- It's a simple question, Mr. Johnston,

00:04:53.880 --> 00:04:58.173
do you contend that it's not
targeted towards children?

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:02.420
- Clearly and absolutely.
But I can explain to you--

00:05:02.420 --> 00:05:03.680
- [Narrator] The Facebook
documents published

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:04.700
by the Wall Street Journal

00:05:04.700 --> 00:05:06.810
show that the company has
been thinking of more ways

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:08.490
to engage children.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:09.447
One slide reads,

00:05:09.447 --> 00:05:13.350
"leverage play dates to
increase growth/retention".

00:05:13.350 --> 00:05:15.710
In her testimony to
Congress, Frances Haugen,

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:16.930
the Facebook whistleblower,

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.130
who gathered the documents that
informed the Journal series

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.020
made her perspective clear.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:24.940
- They have to make sure
that the next generation

00:05:24.940 --> 00:05:27.673
is just as engaged in
Instagram as the current one.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.700
And the way they'll do
that is by making sure

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:33.180
that children establish habits

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:35.075
before they have good self-regulation.

00:05:35.075 --> 00:05:35.937
- By hooking kids?

00:05:35.937 --> 00:05:37.190
- By hooking kids.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:38.900
- The Facebook spokesman said companies

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.160
that operate in a highly
competitive space,

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.610
including the Wall Street Journal,

00:05:42.610 --> 00:05:45.760
make efforts to appeal
to younger generations.

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.840
Since those Big Tobacco
hearings in the 90s,

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:49.800
we've seen regulation and settlements

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.260
that have limited the use
and marketing of these,

00:05:52.260 --> 00:05:54.020
especially to kids.

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.350
The Master Settlement Agreement of 1998

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:59.840
banned advertising targeted
at those younger than 18

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:03.150
and banned the use of
cartoons like Joe Camel.

00:06:03.150 --> 00:06:04.870
Ultimately in 2009,

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:08.360
the FDA was given the right
to regulate tobacco products,

00:06:08.360 --> 00:06:10.280
including the levels of nicotine.

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:14.420
- A very similar set
of parallel regulations

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:18.750
have to be put on the box
in order to protect children

00:06:18.750 --> 00:06:23.060
from Instagram, from social
media in the same way

00:06:23.060 --> 00:06:24.850
that our nation put laws on the box

00:06:24.850 --> 00:06:27.470
to protect children against Big Tobacco.

00:06:27.470 --> 00:06:29.800
- [Narrator] That's
democratic Senator Ed Markey.

00:06:29.800 --> 00:06:32.940
He has drafted the Kids
Internet Design and Safety Act,

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:34.290
or the KIDS Act.

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.850
It aims to create new
protections for users under 16.

00:06:37.850 --> 00:06:39.410
They get to the heart of what's addicting

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:40.600
about social media.

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:43.630
- We have to ban push notification.

00:06:43.630 --> 00:06:46.200
We have to ban auto-play.

00:06:46.200 --> 00:06:49.680
We have to make sure that
the algorithms can't be set

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:52.240
so that it's deliberately playing games

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.540
with the minds of children in our country

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:57.340
up to the age of 16.

00:06:57.340 --> 00:06:58.173
- [Narrator] Then there's

00:06:58.173 --> 00:07:01.300
the Children's Online Privacy
Protection Act, or COPPA 2.0,

00:07:01.300 --> 00:07:02.970
which is also backed by Markey

00:07:02.970 --> 00:07:05.010
and has gained bipartisan support.

00:07:05.010 --> 00:07:07.070
It would extend the
current privacy protections

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:09.040
to 13 through 15 year olds,

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.720
ban targeted advertising to kids and more.

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.790
Beyond protecting the
children, others argue

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:16.630
for regulating social
media content algorithms

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.660
and adjusting section 230

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.320
the law that shields online
platforms from being liable

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:24.040
for harmful content posted on their sites.

00:07:24.040 --> 00:07:25.940
But even if Congress agrees on all that,

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:28.250
it will still have to
contend with lobbyists

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.120
as it did with Big Tobacco.

00:07:30.120 --> 00:07:32.420
- Facebook and other
social media companies,

00:07:32.420 --> 00:07:35.870
they can buy just about every
lobbyist in Washington DC.

00:07:35.870 --> 00:07:40.700
But when the subject of
children, of teenagers is raised,

00:07:40.700 --> 00:07:42.850
they're in a position
which is indefensible.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:44.570
- The Facebook spokesman
said that the company

00:07:44.570 --> 00:07:46.457
is in support of regulation.

00:07:46.457 --> 00:07:48.830
"It's time for Congress to act." He said.

00:07:48.830 --> 00:07:50.790
This all now sits with the lawmakers.

00:07:50.790 --> 00:07:53.520
And if tobacco regulation is our guide,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.420
it could be with them for a long time.

00:07:56.703 --> 00:07:59.823
(narrator sniffing) I reek of tobacco.

